Field Notes: Planting Garlic in Autumn
======================================

Garlic is one of the easiest crops to grow, but it rewards a little
planning. Plant cloves in mid-October, roughly six weeks before the ground
freezes, so the roots can settle in before winter. Choose firm, healthy
bulbs from a local grower rather than supermarket garlic, which is often
treated to stop it sprouting.

Break the bulbs into cloves the day you plant them. Set each clove pointy
end up, about 5 cm deep and 15 cm apart, in rows 30 cm apart. Cover the bed
with a 10 cm layer of straw or shredded leaves; it keeps the soil from
heaving during freeze-thaw cycles and smothers early weeds.

In spring, remove most of the mulch once the shoots are up and feed the bed
with compost. Hardneck varieties send up curly flower stalks ("scapes") in
early summer -- cut them off and eat them, so the plant puts its energy into
the bulb instead.

Harvest when the lower third of the leaves have turned brown, usually in
July. Lift the bulbs with a fork, brush off the soil and hang them in bunches
somewhere dry and airy for 3-4 weeks. Keep the biggest, best-formed bulbs
for next year's planting.

Checklist:
  * order seed garlic by September
  * prepare the bed with compost
  * plant, water once, mulch
  * cut scapes in June; harvest in July

Questions or tips? Write to notes@example.com or visit
https://notes.example.com/garlic. Happy planting!
